# Handling Requests and Responses

Our server so far answers every request the same way, which no real server does. Visiting the home page and visiting a page that does not exist should not produce the same reply. To tell them apart we need the **request** object; to answer properly we need more of the **response** object.

```javascript
const http = require('http');

const server = http.createServer((request, response) => {
  // request:  what the client asked for
  // response: how we answer
});

server.listen(3000);
```

## What Is in the Request

The request object describes the incoming message. Two properties matter most:

- **`request.url`** is the path that was asked for.
- **`request.method`** is `GET`, `POST`, and so on. That is the next lesson's subject.

You can watch `url` for yourself:

```javascript
const server = http.createServer((request, response) => {
  console.log('request to: ' + request.url);

  response.end('Hello client');
});
```

Visit `http://localhost:3000` and it logs `/`. Visit `http://localhost:3000/about` and it logs `/about`. The host and port are not included, because the server already knows those; what arrives is the path.

## Routing on the URL

That is enough to answer different requests differently:

```javascript
const server = http.createServer((request, response) => {
  if (request.url === '/') {
    response.end('Welcome home');
  } else if (request.url === '/about') {
    response.end('About us');
  } else {
    response.end('Page not found');
  }
});
```

Deciding what to do based on the requested URL is called **routing**, and every web framework you ever meet is an elaboration of that `if` statement.

## What Is in the Response

`response.end(text)` sends a body and closes the connection. There is more to a response than the body, and one method covers it:

**`response.writeHead(statusCode, headers)`** sends the response **header**: a status code summarizing what happened, and a set of headers describing the response.

```javascript
response.writeHead(404, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
response.end('Page not found');
```

Two separate pieces of information there.

**The status code** tells the client what happened. Our third branch above sent the text "Page not found" with no status at all, so as far as the browser was concerned the request **succeeded** and the page's content happens to read "Page not found". A search engine would index it. `404` is how you actually say it is missing.

**`Content-Type`** tells the client how to interpret the body. `text/plain` means show it as text; `text/html` means parse it as HTML. Get this wrong and the browser displays your HTML as visible tags, or tries to render your plain text as markup.

`writeHead` goes **before** `end`, because the header is sent before the body.

Also on the response object: **`response.write(text)`** sends part of a body without closing the connection, so you can build a response in pieces and then call `end`.

Here is the whole handler, with headers:

```javascript
const server = http.createServer((request, response) => {
  if (request.url === '/') {
    response.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    response.end('Welcome home');
  } else if (request.url === '/about') {
    response.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    response.end('About us');
  } else {
    response.writeHead(404, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    response.end('Page not found');
  }
});
```

## Running It Yourself

This one is on disk too, as `demo-server/routing-server.js`:

```{walkthrough}
title: demo-server/routing-server.js
file: demo-server/routing-server.js
region: handler
The whole server is one function with one `if` statement in it. Each branch sends a status code and a content type, then a body.
@"console.log": Logs the method and the path of every request, so the terminal shows you exactly what the browser asked for. Load the page once and watch the extra request for `/favicon.ico` arrive on its own.
@"request.url === '/about'": Add another branch here for `/contact` and restart the server to see it answer.
@"writeHead(404": The status code is the part that matters. Change this `404` to `200`, restart, and the "Page not found" text still appears, except now the browser believes the request succeeded.
```

Run it the same way as before, stopping any server already using port 3000 first:

```
$ cd "demo-server"
$ node routing-server.js
Listening on port 3000
```

Now visit each of `http://localhost:3000`, `http://localhost:3000/about`, and `http://localhost:3000/xyz`. Three different answers from one handler. Open the Network tab before loading the last one and you can see the `404` in the status column and `text/plain` among the response headers, which is the same information the code above wrote.

## Status Codes

`200` and `404` are two of many. They come in families, and the first digit tells you the family:

| Code | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `200` | OK, the request succeeded |
| `301`, `302` | Redirect; the thing is somewhere else |
| `400` | Bad request; the client sent something malformed |
| `403` | Forbidden; you are not allowed this |
| `404` | Not found |
| `500` | Server error; something broke on our side |

`404` is the one everybody has seen. `200` is the one everybody has seen far more often without noticing, because when a request succeeds you look at the page rather than at the code.

The Network tab is where you check this on a real page: every request is listed with its status code and its response headers, `Content-Type` among them.

## Running a Handler Here

Again, no ports in a notebook, so we call the handler ourselves. This fake response records what a real one would have sent:

```{node autorun}
function makeFakeResponse() {
  const sent = { statusCode: null, headers: null, body: '' };

  sent.writeHead = function (code, headers) {
    sent.statusCode = code;
    sent.headers = headers;
  };

  sent.end = function (text) {
    sent.body = text;
  };

  return sent;
}

function handleRequest(request, response) {
  if (request.url === '/') {
    response.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    response.end('Welcome home');
  } else if (request.url === '/about') {
    response.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    response.end('About us');
  } else {
    response.writeHead(404, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    response.end('Page not found');
  }
}

for (const url of ['/', '/about', '/nowhere']) {
  const response = makeFakeResponse();

  handleRequest({ url: url }, response);

  console.log(url + ' -> ' + response.statusCode + ' ' + response.body);
}
```

Three requests, three different answers, from the same handler.

## Exercises

1. Write a handler that serves a home page and a contact page, and reports anything else as missing. Send plain text in every case.

    ```{node}
    function makeFakeResponse() {
      const sent = { statusCode: null, headers: null, body: '' };

      sent.writeHead = function (code, headers) {
        sent.statusCode = code;
        sent.headers = headers;
      };

      sent.end = function (text) {
        sent.body = text;
      };

      return sent;
    }

    function handleRequest(request, response) {
      // TODO:
      //   '/'        -> 200, body 'Welcome home'
      //   '/contact' -> 200, body 'Contact us'
      //   anything else -> 404, body 'Page not found'
      //   Always send a Content-Type of text/plain
    }

    const home = makeFakeResponse();
    handleRequest({ url: '/' }, home);

    console.log(home.statusCode + ' ' + home.body);
    ```
    ```+solution
    function makeFakeResponse() {
      const sent = { statusCode: null, headers: null, body: '' };

      sent.writeHead = function (code, headers) {
        sent.statusCode = code;
        sent.headers = headers;
      };

      sent.end = function (text) {
        sent.body = text;
      };

      return sent;
    }

    function handleRequest(request, response) {
      if (request.url === '/') {
        response.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
        response.end('Welcome home');
      } else if (request.url === '/contact') {
        response.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
        response.end('Contact us');
      } else {
        response.writeHead(404, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
        response.end('Page not found');
      }
    }

    const home = makeFakeResponse();
    handleRequest({ url: '/' }, home);

    console.log(home.statusCode + ' ' + home.body);
    ```
    ```+test
    wrap(home.statusCode).should('equal', 200).run('A request for `/` should get a `200` status through `response.writeHead`', 'The home page returns 200');
    wrap(home.body).should('equal', 'Welcome home').run('The body for `/` should be exactly `Welcome home`', 'The home page body is right');
    wrap(home.headers && home.headers['Content-Type']).should('equal', 'text/plain').run('Include a `Content-Type` of `text/plain` in the header', 'The home page sets a content type');
    wrap((function () { const r = makeFakeResponse(); handleRequest({ url: '/contact' }, r); return r.statusCode + '|' + r.body; })()).should('equal', '200|Contact us').run('A request for `/contact` should get `200` and the body `Contact us`', 'The contact page works');
    wrap((function () { const r = makeFakeResponse(); handleRequest({ url: '/nowhere' }, r); return r.statusCode + '|' + r.body; })()).should('equal', '404|Page not found').run('Anything else should get `404` and the body `Page not found`. Without the status code the browser thinks the request succeeded', 'Unknown URLs return 404');
    ```

2. You want to know what path the client asked for, so that `localhost:3000/contact` can be answered differently from `localhost:3000`. Which property do you check?

    ```{mcq}
    Which property of the request holds the path?
    correct: Correct! `request.url` is the path, `/contact` in that example. The host and port are not part of it, since the server already knows them.
    wrong: Only one of these is a real property of Node's request object.
    [x] `request.url`
    [ ] `request.domain`
    [ ] `request.pathway`
    [ ] `request.info`
    ```

3. Which line sends a successful status code along with a plain-text content type?

    ```{mcq}
    Pick the line that sends that header.
    correct: Correct! `writeHead` takes the status code first and a headers object second, and `200` is success.
    wrong: The status code and the headers are two separate arguments to one method, and the body is sent afterwards.
    [x] `response.writeHead(200, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });`
    [ ] `response.writeHead(404, { 'Content-Type': 'text/html' });`
    [ ] `response.end('200 OK - text/plain');`
    [ ] `http.createServer(200);`
    ```

